Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of porous crystalline materials with large surface areas, regular pore sizes, and tailorable surface chemistry,[1] have received considerable attention in a variety of fields.[2-8] For many applications such as separations,[3], energy storage[5] and conversion,[6] chemical sensing,[7] and electronics,[8] it is often necessary to process MOF materials as films on a specific substrate. Among methods established for preparing MOF films,[9,10] strategies based on electrochemical synthesis are attractive for many advantages including technological flexibility, scalable preparation, and industrial production potential.[11] Nevertheless, their potentiality on preparing MOF films with precisely tunable morphology and thickness has not been fully explored. These variables, however, are critically important for structure integration and performance optimization in (especially device) applications.[8,9]
MOFs could be deposited on the electrode surface anodically[12] or cathodically.[13] In cathodic deposition, reduction of some special molecules or ions (e.g. nitrate ions) on electrode surface results in an increase in pH, which promotes the deprotonation of organic ligands and induces the formation of MOFs. At first glance, cathodic deposition appears to be attractive for the direct synthesis of MOFs on conductive substrates. However, reports on the cathodic deposition of MOFs are comparably few. The challenge arises from the more negative reduction potential of nitrate ions relative to those of some important metal ions, e.g. Zn(II), Co(II), and Cu(II), which constitute the most extensively studied MOFs.[13b,g] As a result, cathodic deposition of these MOF materials is commonly accompanied with plating of the corresponding metals,[13a,e] which is in most cases undesirable.
Here, we report the first cathodic deposition of zeolitic imidazolate framework materials (ZIFs), an important subgroup of MOFs that have been extensively studied due to their outstanding thermal stability and chemical robustness.[14,15] Our strategy is based on an oxygen reduction triggered-electrochemicalchemical-reaction scheme responsible for the formation of ZIF materials (Scheme 1). Electrochemical oxygen reduction has been intensively studied due to its importance in energy conversion and storage[16] in addition to its critical role in elucidating superoxide ion-relevant biological processes.[17] Recently, cathodic synthesis of MOF-5 in the presence of molecular oxygen was investigated, but the formation of metallic Zn was still observed during the deposition process (presumably due to the very negative reduction potential (-1.5 V) used).[18] We found that, under optimized conditions, crystalline materials of ZIF-8, ZIF-71, and ZIF-67 could be cathodically synthesized without the plating of metals (Zn or Co). This electrochemical process renders a flexible control over the formation of ZIF single crystals or uniform films with tunable thickness on the nanometer scale. We also show that the presence of supporting electrolytes does not influence the porosity of the deposited ZIF materials, but the deposition can be carried out without supporting electrolytes. Furthermore, we demonstrate its combinations with conformal growth of different ZIF materials and templating strategy for creating well-defined submicrometer-sized heterogeneous structures within ZIF films.
The cathodic deposition of ZIF materials was undertaken at room temperature in the air-saturated precursor solutions of $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ (or $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2})$ and imidazole ligands in methanol. The $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ concentration in methanolic solutions was estimated at ${\sim}2~\mathsf{m M}$ .[19] We first examined potentiostatically the formation of ZIF-8 on the freshly peeled highly oriented pyrolytic graphite $({\mathsf{H O P G}})$ cathode in the presence of supporting electrolyte (tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate $((N B u_{4})P F_{6}))$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements showed that no obvious product was formed on the HOPG surface upon applying a potential of $-0.5\vee$ vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ (3 M KCl) for $2\ h$ (Figure S1). However, scattered irregularly-shaped particles with sizes of
$100{\sim}300~\mathsf{n m}$ appeared on the electrode surface after $2h$ when potential was decreased to $-0.7\:\mathsf{V}$ (Figure 1a). The particles grew larger $(\sim400\ \mathsf{n m})$ and evolved to rhombic dodecahedral crystals after 6 h (Figure 1b). Prolonging reaction time (12 h) further increased the sizes $(500\sim600\:\mathrm{nm})$ of the crystals which, however, appeared to commonly exist in intergrown form (Figure 1c). As indicated by the $\mathsf{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) analysis (Figure 1d), materials formed on HOPG surface were crystalline and displayed similar diffraction patterns which are characteristic for ZIF-8.[14] Morphologies of the deposited crystals could be tuned further by applying more negative potentials. Crystals were found to intergrow fully and cover the HOPG surface completely at -0.9 V (Figure 1e). The domain sizes of the intergrown crystals $(200{\sim}300\mathsf{n m}$ for $-0.9V)$ changed smaller as decreasing potential 1 $(40\sim80\mathsf{n m}$ for -1.0 V) (Figure 1f), reflecting a promoted nucleation of ZIF-8 on the electrode surface. In addition, we noted that no solid product was observed in the reaction solution during synthesis and following the deposition process, suggesting that reactions involved in the formation of ZIF-8 occur rapidly near the electrode.
To gain insight into the mechanism of the cathodic formation of ZIF-8, electrochemical behaviors of solvent, salt solution, ligand solution, and precursor solution were studied by cyclic voltammetry (CV) on a glass carbon electrode (GCE) (see the details in Supporting Information). In the absence of molecular oxygen (Figure 1 and Figure S2a), methanol, HmiM, and $Z n(11)$ in the precursor solution were proved cathodically stable within the investigated potential range of $-1.2-1.0\vee$ . The reduction of $Z n(11)$ occurred at $\neg1.33\lor$ in the precursor solution (Figure S3) and -1.08 V in the $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ solution (Figure S2a), respectively. The distinct shift in reduction potential of $Z n(11)$ could be attributed to complexation of $Z n(11)$ with HmiM (Figure S4), forming $[Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{H}\mathsf{m i}\mathsf{M})_{\mathsf{m}}\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{n}}]$ $(\mathsf{L}=\mathsf{C}\mathsf{I^{-}}$ and methanol, $1\leq\mathsf{m}\geq4$ , and $0\leq\mathsf{n}\geq3^{\prime}$ ), which decreased the activity of free metal ions.
When the solutions were saturated with air, cathodic responses arising from the reduction of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{O}_{2}\mathsf{^{-}}$ on the electrode surface (reaction 1) were observed (Figure S2b).[20] Compared with those in the background solution and HmiM solution $(\sim-0.4$ V), onset potentials of oxygen reduction were observed to shift positively in the $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ solution (-0.16 V) and the precursor solution $(-0.30\vee)$ . Similar results have been reported previously[21-23] and $Z n(11)$ was demonstrated as a Lewis acid to facilitate the reduction of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ to $\bar{\mathsf{O}}_{2}\bar{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ at more positive potentials and form superoxo-Zn(II) complex (reaction 2). In addition, two cathodic peaks were observed at $-0.7\:\mathsf{V}$ and $\mathbf{\Pi}^{-1.0\ V}$ for the precursor solution in the first scan. However, they disappeared in subsequent scans and the cathodic current decreased steadily (Figure S7b). These observations suggest the adsorption and precipitation of materials on the electrode surface. Accordingly, cathodic peaks observed in the first scan may correspond to the reduction of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ on the electrode surface (-0.7 V) and likely on the formed “film” (-1.0 V), respectively. The absence of anodic responses in the potential range of $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.-1.2-0\aftergroup\egroup\right\vee$ in the inverse scan indicates that metallic Zn was not formed in the cathodic scan and the rapid disproportionation of ${O_{2}}^{-}$ occurred in the methanolic precursor solution. It is known that the superoxide ion ${O_{2}}^{-}$ or superoxo-Zn(II) complex reacts with a proton or proton donors to yield $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and hydrogen peroxide.[20,24] Compared with the free imidazoles, moderately decreased $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ values have been suggested for their corresponding metal-complexes.[25,26] Given the stronger acidity of $[Z n(H\min)_{\max}]$ $(\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}=10.3$ in aqueous solutions[26,27]) relative to those of the free HmiM (14.2) and methanol (15.5), we therefore speculated that the superoxo-Zn(II) intermediates were presumably inclined to react with $Z n(11)$ -imidazole complexes (reaction 3). The deprotonated complex $([Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{m i M})(\mathsf{H}\mathsf{m i M})_{\mathsf{m}-1}\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{n}}])$ subsequently evolved to ZIF-8 via ligand-exchange, nucleation, and growth as demonstrated by Wiebcke et al.[27]
