The growth kinetics of ZIF-8 films were investigated on indium tin oxide (ITO) electrode under cyclic voltammetry (CV) mode (- 1.0 – 0 V, $100\mathrm{mV/s},$ ). We first carried out the deposition with the supporting electrolyte-containing solution of $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ $(10\ m M)$ and HmiM $(20\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M})$ . SEM measurements revealed the uniform surface morphology of the deposited films and their time-related thickness (Figure S10). The average film thicknesses were 24 and $172\:\mathsf{n m}$ for deposition times of 15 and $600~\mathrm{{min}}$ , respectively. The crystal domain sizes increased from $15\sim30\:\mathrm{nm}$ (15 min) to $\sim150\:\mathrm{nm}$ (600 min) correspondingly. The films grew rapidly in the first $30~\mathrm{min}$ (with an average rate of $1.47\ \mathrm{nm/min})$ but thickened slowly (by $0.21\ \mathrm{nm/min})$ after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure 3a) and stopped growing after 600 min likely due to the slow diffusion of reactive species through the formed nonconductive framework layer. The electrodeposition also can be performed in the supporting electrolyte-free precursor solution containing 10 mM $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ and $30~\mathsf{m M}$ HmiM, where, however, a faster growth of ZIF-8 films was observed under similar experimental conditions (Figure 3b and Figure S11). The films thickened by ${\sim}9.2\ \mathrm{nm/min}$ in the first 5 min and by ${\sim}0.49$ $\mathsf{n m/m i n}$ after $120~\mathrm{\min}$ , respectively (Figure 3a). Control experiments indicate that the $Z\mathsf{n C l}_{2}$ Hmim molar ratio in precursor solutions had an important effect on the growth kinetics of ZIF-8 films during electrodeposition (Figure S12 and S13). In the precursor solution with a $Z n C l_{2}/H_{\min}$ molar ratio of 1:2, both nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 films depended on the electrochemistry. In the precursor solution with a $Z n C l_{2}/H\min$ molar ratio of 1:3, the nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 in the first 5 min were dominated by electrochemistry, whereas the thickening of film after 120 min was contributed substantially by the seedinduced growth which was irrelevant to electrochemistry. As confirmed by CV curves recorded during the deposition (Figure S14) and XRD analysis (Figure 3c), metallic Zn has not been produced during the formation of ZIF-8 films.
The porosity of evacuated ZIF-8 films on ITO substrate was confirmed by krypton-adsorption measurements.[10] As shown in Figure 4a, all the ZIF-8 thin film samples display type I isotherms with steep increases in krypton uptake at low relative pressure $_{(<0.01)}$ , indicating their microporosity. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area, expressed as per film area per $100~\mathsf{n m}$ film thickness, was calculated as 1 $|91~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{m}^{2}/100~\mathsf{n m}$ for ZIF-8 film deposited in the presence of supporting electrolyte for $10\mathsf{h}$ and 179, 175, 179, and $178~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{m}^{2}/100~\mathrm{nm}$ for samples obtained by 2, 4, 6, and $^{8\mathfrak{h}}$ of electrolyte-free depositions, respectively (Figure 4b). These values coincide, within experimental uncertainty, to that of a 415 nm ZIF-8 film ( $184~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{m}^{2}/100~\mathsf{n m})$ solvothermally grown[28] on an ITO substrate (Figure S16). The above results also imply that when supporting electrolytes were applied in the cathodic deposition, they were not entrapped within cavities of the formed ZIF materials. The absence of electrolyte $((N B u_{4})P F_{6})$ in the deposited ZIF-8 films was also confirmed by time-of-flight secondary ionization mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) analysis (Figure S17). The pore nature of the electrosynthesized ZIF-8 films was further evaluated by deposition onto the HOPG substrate and serving as a Fabry-Pérot chemical sensor.[28] The sensing study showed that the electrochemically fabricated device displayed negligible response to water vapor, which is consistent with the hydrophobicity of ZIF-8.[14] However, it was responsive to methanol vapor with the similar thermodynamic and kinetic adsorption-desorption behaviors as the solvothermallyprepared counterpart (Figure S18).
The cathodic deposition process was also applied successfully to ZIF-71 and ZIF-67 under similar experimental conditions as ZIF-8. The former is built from $Z n(11)$ ions bridged by 4,5-dichloroimidazole (dclM) and the latter consists of Co(II) ions linked by HmiM.[14] Likewise, the plating of metallic Zn or Co did not occur during the deposition of these two ZIF materials as confirmed by XRD analysis (Figure S20 and S21).
The controllable growth of ZIF materials facilitates greatly the introduction of well-defined heterogeneous structures in the deposited films. Figure 5a shows a photograph of a transparent hybrid film $(\sim5\times7~\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ prepared on ITO substrate by the CV deposition of ZIF-8 first for one hour and then the seed-induced growth of ZIF-67 for another hour. The thickness of film was $\sim100$ nm after the first deposition (Figure 5b) and increased to ${\sim}300\ \mathsf{n m}$ after the second deposition (Figure 5c). Figure 5d shows a transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of one fragment of the film that was carefully removed from ITO substrate. This fragment was ${\sim}300$ nm in thickness, which is consistent with SEM measurements for the pristine film, and had a smooth interface originally contacting the surface of ITO substrate and a relatively rough interface formed at the end of deposition. Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) mapping revealed that organic ligands (HmiM) were distributed homogeneously in the whole sample whereas Co and Zn were confined separately within a $\mathord{\sim}200$ nm layer and a $\sim100\ \mathsf{n m}$ layer, respectively, indicating the welldefined bilayer structure in the ZIF-8/ZIF-67 hybrid film (Figure 5e). Alternatively, when the electrode surface was coated with a selfassembled monolayer of polystyrene microspheres, ZIF materials could be grown through the interstices of microspheres and finally cover the template completely (Figure 5f). Removal of the microsphere template yielded ordered macropores in the microporous framework films (Figure 5g). High resolution TEM measurements (Figure 5h) and XRD analysis (Figure S23) established that the macroporous film comprised ZIF-8.
In summary, we have developed a facile and cost-effective cahtodic method that enables the deposition of large-area uniform ZIF films with tuneable thickness but without the plating of metals (Zn and Co). This oxygen-assisted cathodic synthesis strategy shows great promise for other MOF materials consisting of other easily reduced metal ions, such as $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ . This work also demonstrates the applicability of electrochemical strategies for preparing MOF films with controllable mesoscopic features.
